FAQ_HEADING = "Frequently Asked Questions"
FAQS = [
    ("2. Are dental X-rays safe? How much radiation do they use?", "Yes — dental X-rays are extremely safe when clinically justified. Australian dental X-ray units must comply with ARPANSA's Code of Practice for Radiation Protection in Dentistry. One bitewing exposes you to about 0.005 mSv, an OPG around 0.01–0.03 mSv, and a small-field CBCT roughly 0.01–0.1 mSv. For context, the average Australian receives 1.5–2.5 mSv per year from natural background radiation alone. Dentists follow the ALARA principle — As Low As Reasonably Achievable."),
    ("3. How often do I really need dental X-rays?", "It depends on your risk level, not the calendar. Low-risk adults need bitewings every 2–3 years and an OPG every 2–5 years if clinically indicated. High-risk adults — those with active decay, dry mouth or multiple existing fillings — may need bitewings every 6–18 months. Children and adolescents typically need bitewings every 12–18 months depending on caries risk. CBCT is reserved for specific clinical questions that 2D imaging cannot answer."),
    ("4. Are dental X-rays covered by Medicare or private health insurance?", "Medicare does not cover dental X-rays in private practice; it only covers dento-maxillofacial imaging in public hospitals. Private health extras funds generally cover 60–100% of diagnostic X-rays depending on your level of cover and waiting periods. Eligible children aged 2–17 can have X-rays covered under the Child Dental Benefits Schedule. Always ask the practice to check your eligibility before the appointment."),
    ("5. Can I say no to dental X-rays?", "Yes, you can. Informed consent applies to dental radiographs just like any other procedure. However, if you refuse an X-ray that is clinically necessary — for example, to diagnose a toothache or plan a root canal — the dentist is entitled to decline non-emergency treatment that relies on that image. A good dentist will explain exactly why the X-ray is needed and what the risk of not taking it might be."),
    ("6. Are dental X-rays safe during pregnancy?", "Australian guidelines recommend postponing non-urgent dental X-rays during pregnancy. If an X-ray is clinically necessary — for example, for a dental abscess — it can be taken with appropriate shielding (thyroid collar and lead apron) and minimal exposure. The radiation dose of a single dental X-ray is far below levels associated with any proven harm to a developing fetus. Always tell your dentist if you are pregnant or suspect you may be."),
    ("7. What is the difference between an OPG and a CBCT scan?", "An OPG (orthopantomogram) is a 2D panoramic image of your whole mouth, showing all teeth, sinuses and jaw joints on a single film. A CBCT (cone-beam computed tomography) scan produces a 3D image of a specific region, showing tooth roots, nerves and bone in cross-section. OPGs are used for general screening and wisdom tooth assessment; CBCT is the modern standard for implant planning, complex extractions and tricky root canal anatomy. CBCT costs more and delivers a slightly higher radiation dose."),
    ("8. Do children need dental X-rays? Is it covered by the Child Dental Benefits Schedule?", "Children with a history of decay, or those entering mixed dentition, may need bitewing X-rays every 12–18 months to catch decay between teeth early. The Child Dental Benefits Schedule covers X-rays for eligible children aged 2–17, with a benefit cap of approximately $1,026 per child over two consecutive calendar years — verify the current indexed rate on the Services Australia website. Parental consent is required before any X-ray of a child is taken."),
]

